<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.6.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00952</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.6.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>25cefbb4-21e5-4134-9622-778cd6f10f7d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_2edfb5a6-2a2d-4b15-aba3-7fcdc835192b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>This graph represents Andre’s distance from his bicycle as he walks in a park.</para>
            <para>Decide whether the following statements are true or false.</para>
            <para>The graph has multiple horizontal intercepts.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! True</para>
            <para>Try again. Take a moment to think about what you learned in activit ies 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is True.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7bfa27ce-1a34-45fc-ae81-c6b62d2c7364" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A horizontal intercept of the graph represents the time when Andre was with his bike.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! True</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is True.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_36e11728-cc0e-4542-928c-89aa46d9aaa6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A maximum of the graph is the point (17,1)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is false.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a404b2e6-3119-433d-a9a3-4392b006b4bb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>The graph represents the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, as a function of time.</para>
            <para>At what hour in the afternoon does the maximum of the graph occur? Hint: Do not enter commas or spaces in your answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 3 p.m.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3 p.m.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_50cd62ca-0eb7-4cb5-b9b1-1e205ac79cec" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Match each feature of the situation with a corresponding statement in function notation.</para>
            <para>\(h(0)= 7\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Starting height</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.6.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Starting height.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4321f5b0-5277-42cb-8710-3a193370112f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(h(1.5)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Minimum height</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.6.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Minimum height.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f8827eaa-9c81-457c-90b0-04d36a637ab7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(h(4)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Maximum height</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.6.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Maximum height.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2d581c61-654a-4e65-b025-52d8746edc03" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\(h(t)=6\) for \(7 ≤t ≤8\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Height staying the same</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.6.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Height staying the same.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c322ec48-07c3-4c29-8e66-2583a3cf0c54" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>The graph of \(f(x) =x^2\) is provided.</para>
            <para>What is \(f(2)\)?

         </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!  The value of \(f(x)\) when \(x\) is 2 or \(f(2) = 4\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is the value of \(f(x)\) when \(x\) is 2 or \(f(2) = 4\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6b79e68e-ba10-4f57-bb67-d3dfe1fadd9f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the value of \(f(1)\)?

         </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(f(1) = 1\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(f(1) = 1\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_871059dc-c8af-4dff-af07-3b73008398b5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Shannon is running on a flat, rocky trail that eventually rises up a steep mountain. The graph below represents the total distance she covers with respect to time. 
          
          <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_09_13_01.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>How many miles does Shannon run in 60 minutes?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 3 miles</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.6.2-4.6.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3 miles.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
